//? Title: Acceptance sampling by attributes
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Design a single sampling plan, which inspects n items and accepts the lot if at most c are nonconforming, so that good lots are rarely rejected and bad lots rarely accepted, and see what the plan delivers under rectifying inspection

A single sampling plan \((n,c)\) is judged by its **operating characteristic** (OC) curve: the probability \(P_a(p)\) of accepting a lot whose fraction nonconforming is \(p\). The design fixes two points on that curve:

- the *producer's risk point*: lots at the acceptable quality level (AQL) should be accepted with probability at least \(1-\alpha\);
- the *consumer's risk point*: lots at the rejectable quality level (RQL, or LTPD) should be accepted with probability at most \(\beta\).

The number of nonconforming items in the sample is hypergeometric for an isolated lot of size \(N\) (a type A OC), binomial for lots from a continuing process (type B), and Poisson when counting defects per unit. Under *rectifying inspection*, rejected lots are screened 100% and nonconforming items replaced. The plan then determines the average outgoing quality \(AOQ(p)=pP_a(p)(N-n)/N\), its worst case (the AOQL), and the average total inspection \(ATI=n+(1-P_a)(N-n)\).

`acceptance_probability` evaluates the OC under each model. `find_attribute_plan` finds the smallest plan meeting both risk points, using the search of `AcceptanceSampling::find.plan`. `rectifying_performance` and `average_outgoing_quality_limit` give the AOQ, ATI and AOQL. The tests reproduce `AcceptanceSampling` plans for 24 risk-point combinations under all three models, and its OC values to \(10^{-12}\). They check that no smaller n meets the risk points, and compare AOQ, ATI and AOQL with direct computation in R.

The example designs a plan for AQL 1% (\(\alpha=5\%\)) and RQL 6% (\(\beta=10\%\)), and compares it with the zero-acceptance-number plan that meets only the consumer point. A simulation of 20000 lots of 1000 checks the realized risks. Lots come from a stable process at the AQL or the RQL, or from a process whose lot quality varies (a Beta distribution with mean 1% and SD 1.5%).

```{cpp, id=as, file="figures/acceptance_sampling.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/acceptance_sampling.hpp>

#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::AttributePlan;
using dm::stats::SamplingModel;

// A lot of N items with d nonconforming; inspect n without replacement.
std::size_t sample_defects(dm::random::SplitMix64& rng, std::size_t lot, std::size_t d,
                           std::size_t n) {
    std::size_t found = 0, left = lot, bad = d;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i, --left) {
        if (rng.uniform01() * static_cast<double>(left) < static_cast<double>(bad))
            ++found, --bad;
    }
    return found;
}

std::size_t binomial_draw(dm::random::SplitMix64& rng, std::size_t n, double p) {
    std::size_t s = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) s += rng.uniform01() < p;
    return s;
}

// Gamma variate (Marsaglia-Tsang; shape < 1 by boosting), for Beta = G1 / (G1 + G2).
double gamma_draw(dm::random::SplitMix64& rng, double shape) {
    if (shape < 1)
        return gamma_draw(rng, shape + 1) * std::pow(rng.uniform01(), 1 / shape);
    const double d = shape - 1.0 / 3, c = 1 / std::sqrt(9 * d);
    while (true) {
        const double z = rng.normal(), v = std::pow(1 + c * z, 3);
        if (v > 0 && std::log(rng.uniform01()) < 0.5 * z * z + d - d * v + d * std::log(v))
            return d * v;
    }
}

int main() {
    const double aql = 0.01, alpha = 0.05, rql = 0.06, beta = 0.10;
    std::println("| Model | Lot size | n | c | P(accept) at AQL | P(accept) at RQL |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto row = [&](const char* name, dm::stats::SamplingOptions o, std::string lot) {
        const auto p = dm::stats::find_attribute_plan(aql, alpha, rql, beta, o);
        std::println("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |", name, lot, p.n, p.c,
                     dm::stats::acceptance_probability(p, aql, o),
                     dm::stats::acceptance_probability(p, rql, o));
    };
    row("Binomial (type B)", {}, "-");
    row("Poisson", {.model = SamplingModel::poisson}, "-");
    for (const std::size_t lot : {100uz, 200uz, 500uz, 1000uz, 5000uz})
        row("Hypergeometric (type A)",
            {.model = SamplingModel::hypergeometric, .lot_size = lot}, std::to_string(lot));

    const auto plan = dm::stats::find_attribute_plan(aql, alpha, rql, beta);
    // Zero-acceptance plan meeting only the consumer point.
    AttributePlan zero{1, 0};
    while (dm::stats::acceptance_probability(zero, rql) > beta) ++zero.n;
    const std::size_t lot = 1000;
    std::println("\n| Plan | P(accept) at AQL | P(accept) at RQL | AOQL (at p) | "
                 "ATI at AQL | ATI at RQL |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& p : {plan, zero}) {
        const auto a = dm::stats::average_outgoing_quality_limit(p, lot);
        std::println("| n = {}, c = {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.4f} ({:.3f}) | {:.0f} | "
                     "{:.0f} |", p.n, p.c, dm::stats::acceptance_probability(p, aql),
                     dm::stats::acceptance_probability(p, rql), a.aoql, a.quality,
                     dm::stats::rectifying_performance(p, aql, lot).ati,
                     dm::stats::rectifying_performance(p, rql, lot).ati);
    }

    // Simulation: 20000 lots of 1000 from three processes, inspected with the plan.
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(13);
    const int lots = 20000;
    std::println("\n| Lots from | Accepted (simulated) | Binomial OC | Mean incoming p | "
                 "Mean outgoing p (rectifying) |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const double mean = 0.01, sd = 0.015;  // Beta with this mean and SD
    const double k = mean * (1 - mean) / (sd * sd) - 1, a = mean * k, b = (1 - mean) * k;
    for (int scenario = 0; scenario < 3; ++scenario) {
        double accepted = 0, in = 0, out = 0;
        for (int l = 0; l < lots; ++l) {
            double p = scenario == 0 ? aql : rql;
            if (scenario == 2) {
                const double g1 = gamma_draw(rng, a), g2 = gamma_draw(rng, b);
                p = g1 / (g1 + g2);
            }
            const std::size_t d = binomial_draw(rng, lot, p);
            const bool ok = sample_defects(rng, lot, d, plan.n) <= plan.c;
            accepted += ok;
            in += static_cast<double>(d) / lot / lots;
            // Accepted lots ship their uninspected defects; rejected lots are screened.
            if (ok) {
                const double sampled = static_cast<double>(d) * plan.n / lot;  // expected
                out += (d - sampled) / lot / lots;
            }
        }
        const char* name[3] = {"Process at AQL (1%)", "Process at RQL (6%)",
                               "Varying process (Beta, mean 1%, SD 1.5%)"};
        const double pa = dm::stats::acceptance_probability(plan, scenario ? rql : aql);
        const std::string oc = scenario < 2 ? std::format("{:.4f}", pa) : std::string("-");
        std::println("| {} | {:.4f} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |", name[scenario],
                     accepted / lots, oc, in, out);
    }

    // OC curves.
    std::vector<double> ps, oc1, oc0;
    for (double p = 0; p <= 0.12; p += 0.001) {
        ps.push_back(p);
        oc1.push_back(dm::stats::acceptance_probability(plan, p));
        oc0.push_back(dm::stats::acceptance_probability(zero, p));
    }
    auto fig = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    fig.line(ps, oc1, std::format("n = {}, c = {}", plan.n, plan.c), {26, 90, 147}, 2.5)
        .line(ps, oc0, std::format("n = {}, c = 0", zero.n), {176, 72, 43}, 2)
        .points({aql, rql}, {1 - alpha, beta}, "Risk points", {17, 24, 39}, 7)
        .abline_v(aql, {170, 170, 170})
        .abline_v(rql, {170, 170, 170})
        .y_limits(0, 1.12)
        .title("Operating characteristic curves")
        .x_label("Lot fraction nonconforming p")
        .y_label("Probability of acceptance")
        .size(640, 410);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    fig.save_svg("build/acceptance_sampling.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Model | Lot size | n | c | P(accept) at AQL | P(accept) at RQL |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Binomial (type B) | - | 110 | 3 | 0.9750 | 0.0980 |
| Poisson | - | 112 | 3 | 0.9728 | 0.0976 |
| Hypergeometric (type A) | 100 | 51 | 1 | 1.0000 | 0.0933 |
| Hypergeometric (type A) | 200 | 76 | 2 | 1.0000 | 0.0998 |
| Hypergeometric (type A) | 500 | 83 | 2 | 0.9657 | 0.0973 |
| Hypergeometric (type A) | 1000 | 85 | 2 | 0.9541 | 0.0987 |
| Hypergeometric (type A) | 5000 | 109 | 3 | 0.9772 | 0.0994 |

| Plan | P(accept) at AQL | P(accept) at RQL | AOQL (at p) | ATI at AQL | ATI at RQL |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| n = 110, c = 3 | 0.975 | 0.098 | 0.0157 (0.027) | 132 | 913 |
| n = 38, c = 0 | 0.683 | 0.095 | 0.0092 (0.026) | 343 | 908 |

| Lots from | Accepted (simulated) | Binomial OC | Mean incoming p | Mean outgoing p (rectifying) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Process at AQL (1%) | 0.9738 | 0.9750 | 0.0100 | 0.0086 |
| Process at RQL (6%) | 0.0978 | 0.0980 | 0.0599 | 0.0049 |
| Varying process (Beta, mean 1%, SD 1.5%) | 0.9012 | - | 0.0102 | 0.0053 |
\image(figures/acceptance_sampling.svg)
// result_end

For lots from a continuing process (binomial OC), the risk points require inspecting 110 items and accepting with at most 3 nonconforming. The realized risks are 2.5% at the AQL and 9.8% at the RQL. Plans for isolated lots (hypergeometric) are smaller, because sampling without replacement from a small lot is more informative. For lots of 100 the plan is n = 51 with c = 1: half the lot. Lot size matters only when the sample is a sizeable fraction of the lot; by N = 5000 the plan is essentially the binomial one. The Poisson approximation asks for slightly more (n = 112).

The zero-acceptance plan (n = 38, c = 0) protects the consumer just as well, with 9.5% acceptance at 6%. It is three times smaller, but it rejects 32% of lots at the AQL. Under rectifying inspection that means screening an average of 343 items per lot of 1000 at good quality, against 132 for the c = 3 plan. Its lower AOQL (0.92% against 1.57%) is bought with that extra inspection. The OC curves show why: with c = 0, acceptance falls steeply from p = 0 and there is no shoulder protecting good lots.

The simulation confirms the type B risks: 97.4% of lots from a process at the AQL are accepted (theory 97.5%), and 9.8% at the RQL. When lot quality varies around the same 1% mean, only 90% of lots pass. Sampling preferentially rejects the occasional bad lot, and with rectification the average outgoing quality falls to 0.53%, half the incoming level. Acceptance sampling screens lots; it cannot improve the process that makes them.

>> Practical guidance

- Choose the risk points from consequences: the AQL is the quality you consider acceptable as a process average, and the RQL is quality you must not accept. Then pick the OC model that matches the situation: type A for an isolated lot, type B for a stream of lots from a process.
- Avoid c = 0 plans unless the producer risk at the AQL is acceptable. They are small, but they reject many good lots and force costly screening.
- Under rectifying inspection, report the AOQL and the ATI at the expected process quality, since these drive cost and outgoing quality.
- Treat acceptance sampling as an audit, not quality control. Persistent rejections call for process improvement, with control charts and capability studies, rather than more inspection.

>> References

- H. F. Dodge and H. G. Romig (1959). *Sampling Inspection Tables: Single and Double Sampling*, 2nd ed. Wiley.
- D. C. Montgomery (2019). *Introduction to Statistical Quality Control*, 8th ed., ch. 15. Wiley.
- E. G. Schilling and D. V. Neubauer (2017). *Acceptance Sampling in Quality Control*, 3rd ed. CRC Press. doi:10.4324/9781315120744
- A. Kiermeier (2008). Visualizing and assessing acceptance sampling plans: The R package AcceptanceSampling. *Journal of Statistical Software* 26(6), 1-20. doi:10.18637/jss.v026.i06
- ISO 2859-1:1999. *Sampling procedures for inspection by attributes — Part 1: Sampling schemes indexed by acceptance quality limit (AQL) for lot-by-lot inspection*.
